I'm James Maynard and I'm a math  addition at the University of Oxford  he works on prime numbers all whole  numbers are built up of prime numbers  multiplied together so for  mathematicians like me prime numbers  take this fundamental role of like atoms  that make up all whole numbers but the  really interesting thing is despite them  being these fundamental building blocks  of numbers they're still really poorly  understood and there's lots of basic  questions about them that we haven't  been able to answer even though they've  been studied for thousands of years  I'm always drawn to simple to state  mathematical problems that have  exceptionally difficult and interesting  answers for example one of my favorite  problems in mathematics it's the twin  prime conjecture that asks if there's  infinitely many pairs of primes that  differ by just two this is a really  famous problem that mathematicians have  considered for well over a hundred years  but we still don't know the answer to it  so one problem I worked on was showing  that there's infinitely many prime  numbers that have none of their decimal  digits equal to the number 7 it's a very  rare property for very large numbers to  have no sevens in their decimal  expansion and there's lots of really  important problems about prime numbers  that ask whether there's infinitely many  Prime's that have some rare property so  this is a proof of concept in that  direction  it's an interesting problem because you  can consider a weak variance of the  problem and I'd proved a weaker version  and I've been working really hard to try  and get to the full version of the  answer that I wanted when I finally  solved there I was really happy that I  was able to finally get over this  obstacle was I've been stuck for several  months but at the same time I was  actually a bit concerned because I was  worried that I was going to embarrass  myself in front of everyone if there's a  mistake in my work
 

